-- title:
--   Place-level Eichler–Shimura relation on the non-Gauss component
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime and $M$ a nonzero natural number with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$.
--
--   **The cyclotomic base.** $L$ is a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ for $\{p\}$, and $\zeta \in L$ is a primitive $p$-th root of unity. $A$ is a discrete valuation ring which is a domain with fraction field $L$, such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (hypothesis `hAp`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ (hypothesis `hζA`).
--
--   **The function field and its $j$.** $K$ is an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$, and `hK` requires $K =$ [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of $L$-Laurent series generated by the coefficientwise images of the $q$-expansions in the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. The field $K$ carries an $A$-algebra structure making $A \to L \to K$ a scalar tower. The element $j \in K$ is nonzero and, by `hj`, has Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), the $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-function. All the geometry below takes place over the two-chart integral model [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A (↥K) j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252): the structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ of the scheme obtained by gluing $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the ring [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartAlgFin A (↥K) j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L135) of elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$ with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the corresponding ring for $A[j^{-1}]$ along their common middle chart; this morphism is assumed proper, and [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.ιFin A (↥K) j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L231) denotes the morphism of the finite chart into the model.
--
--   **The fibre in characteristic $p$.** $k$ is an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ and an $A$-algebra. $C_1, C_2$ are schemes with morphisms $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ which are proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, and $i_1, i_2$ are closed immersions of $C_1$ and $C_2$, over $k$, into the base change of the two-chart model to $k$. The hypothesis `hcover` says that every point of that base change lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` says that the scheme-theoretic intersection $C_1 \times_{\,\cdot\,} C_2$ (the pullback of $i_1$ and $i_2$) is reduced; $n$ is its cardinality (`hn`) and $n > 0$ (`hn0`).
--
--   **The generic-fibre model.** The algebraic closure $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ is an $A$- and $L$-algebra with the evident scalar tower. $M_\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) (an integral proper smooth relative-dimension-one curve together with an isomorphism of that field with its function field, compatible with the base, and a bijection between closed points and places), $e_\eta$ is an isomorphism from $M_\eta.C$ onto the base change of the two-chart model to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`). The hypothesis `hMηpin` is the Gauss pinning of $M_\eta$: for every $a$ in the finite chart algebra, the germ at the generic point of the pullback of $a$ along $e_\eta$ followed by the first projection corresponds, under $M_\eta.\mathrm{ffEquiv}^{-1}$, to the element of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` whose Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of that of $a$ under $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; a nonemptiness assumption on the preimage of the finite chart accompanies it. The hypothesis `hgal` is Galois equivariance: for every $g \in \operatorname{Aut}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ fixing the image of $L$ pointwise and all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M_\eta.C$ (sections of $M_\eta.\mathrm{toBase}$), if $x'$ read in the model equals $\operatorname{Spec}(g)$ followed by $x$ read in the model, then $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x' = \mathrm{arithmeticGalois}(g) \cdot M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x$, where [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54) sends $g$ to the semilinear automorphism acting coefficientwise by $g$.
--
--   **The two Igusa readings.** $w$ is an [`ModularCurve.IntegralWeightOneForm k M`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L16): a weight-one modular form for $\Gamma_1(M)$ together with an integral power series which is its $q$-expansion and whose constant-type invariant `intSeriesC` over $k$ is nonzero; [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35) is the associated Igusa function field over $k$. $\mathrm{Mdl}_1$ and $\mathrm{Mdl}_2$ are curve models over $k$ of that field, with isomorphisms $e_1 : \mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$ and $e_2 : \mathrm{Mdl}_2.C \cong C_2$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`he₁`, `he₂`), together with nonemptiness assumptions on the preimages of the finite chart. The hypothesis `hgauss₁` is the Gauss reading of $C_1$: for $a$ in the finite chart algebra and power series $x, y$ over $A$ with $y$ having nonzero reduction to $k$ and $a \cdot y = x$ as $L$-Laurent series, the function field element obtained from $a$ through $e_1$, $i_1$ and the first projection has Laurent series $\bar x/\bar y$ over $k$. The hypothesis `hgauss₂` is the $\sigma$-twisted reading of $C_2$: the same statement with $a$ replaced by $\sigma(a)$ in the relation $\sigma(a) \cdot y = x$.
--
--   **The Atkin–Lehner automorphism.** $\sigma$ is an $L$-algebra automorphism of $K$ with: `hσj`, the Laurent expansion of $\sigma j$ is the coefficientwise image of $j(q^p) =$ `qExpand ℚ p jq`; `hσfin`, $\sigma$ preserves the finite chart algebra in both directions; `hσW`, for every valuation subring $W_0$ of $K$ characterised as the set of quotients $x/y$ of power series over $A$ with $y$ of nonzero residue (the Gauss valuation), the pullback $\sigma^* W_0$ differs from $W_0$, and for every polynomial $P$ over $A$ with nonzero reduction both $P(j)$ and $P(j)^{-1}$ lie in $\sigma^* W_0$; `hσAL`, on the elements of $K$ coming from the full modular function field of level $Mp$, $\sigma$ acts as [`ModularCurve.atkinLehnerInvolutionFull M p`](def/ModularCurve_AtkinLehnerPartial.html#L21); and `hdiamConj`, the diamond conjugation law: for all $d, d'$ coprime to $Mp$ with $d' \equiv d \pmod M$ and $d'd \equiv 1 \pmod p$, and all automorphisms $\theta_d, \theta_{d'}$ of $K$ inducing on Laurent series the base changes to $L$ of the diamond automorphisms `diamondAut (M * p) d` and `diamondAut (M * p) d'`, one has $\sigma \theta_d \sigma^{-1} = \theta_{d'}$ on $K$. Further, $\bar\sigma$ is a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` compatible with $\sigma$ under the coefficientwise map $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hσbar`).
--
--   **Frobenius and Hecke data.** $\mathrm{frobIg}$ is a semilinear automorphism of the Igusa function field over $k$ (a compatible pair consisting of a ring automorphism of the field and one of $k$) whose action raises every Laurent coefficient to the $p$-th power (`hfrobIg`). The hypothesis `hβdef` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeBetaOneDefined (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeOperator.html#L84), namely that $y \mapsto y(q^p)$ maps the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ into `x1x0FunctionFieldC ℚ (M * p) (M * p * p)`; `hα` and `hβ` assert that the two degeneracy maps `heckeAlphaOneBar` and `heckeBetaOneBar` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ are integral ring homomorphisms; a `HasPrincipalDivisors` assumption is made for the intermediate field over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; and `hdeg` asserts that the degree `finrankAlong` of `heckeBetaOneBar` equals $p$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exist a natural number $d$ coprime to $Mp$, a $k$-algebra automorphism $\delta$ of `igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`, and a finite set $S_0$ of places of that field over $k$, such that the following holds for every valuation subring $\mathrm{Pl}$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit in it, every ring homomorphism $\rho : A \to \mathrm{Pl}$ lifting $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, every subring $O$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $O \le \mathrm{Pl}$ and a lift $\rho_O : A \to O$ of $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, every surjective ring homomorphism $\pi_k : \mathrm{Pl} \to k$ with $A \to k$ equal to $\pi_k \circ \rho$, and under the further assumption $\mathrm{Pl} \le O$ (so that $O$ and $\mathrm{Pl}$ coincide as subrings), and for all maps
--   $$\mathrm{red}_1, \mathrm{red}_2 : \mathrm{Place}_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}}(\mathrm{x1FunctionFieldBar}\,(M * p)) \longrightarrow \mathrm{Place}_k(\mathrm{igusaFunctionFieldX1C}\,k\,M\,w)$$
--   satisfying the reduction laws `hred₁` and `hred₂`: for $\mathrm{red}_1$, whenever $P$ is a place, $\xi$ a morphism from $\operatorname{Spec} O$ to the two-chart model over $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho_O)$ whose restriction along $O \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of the model attached to $P$ through $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}^{-1}$, $e_\eta$ and the first projection, and $c$ a $k$-point of $C_1$ whose image in the model is $\operatorname{Spec}(\pi_k \circ (O \le \mathrm{Pl}))$ followed by $\xi$, then $\mathrm{red}_1 P$ is the place of the Igusa field attached by $\mathrm{Mdl}_1.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}$ to $c$ transported through $e_1^{-1}$; and the same law for $\mathrm{red}_2$ with $C_2$, $i_2$, $\mathrm{Mdl}_2$, $e_2$.
--
--   First conjunct (diamond). For all places $P, P_2$ of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` with $P_2 = \mathrm{ofAlgAut}(\mathrm{diamondAutBar}\,(M * p)\,d) \cdot P$: if $P$ reduces into $C_2$, in the sense that there exist $\xi$ over $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho_O)$ and a $k$-point $c$ of $C_2$ such that $\xi$ restricted to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is the point of the model attached to $P$, $c$ read in the model is $\operatorname{Spec}(\pi_k \circ (O \le \mathrm{Pl}))$ followed by $\xi$, no point of $c$ lands in the image of the projection of the intersection $C_1 \times_{\,\cdot\,} C_2$ to $C_2$, and every point of $c$ read in the model lies in the image of the finite chart $\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}}$, then $P_2$ reduces into $C_2$ in exactly the same sense, and
--   $$\mathrm{red}_2 P_2 = \mathrm{ofAlgAut}(\delta) \cdot \mathrm{red}_2 P.$$
--
--   Second conjunct (Eichler–Shimura reading). For all places $P, P_2, Q_p$ with $P_2 = \mathrm{ofAlgAut}(\mathrm{diamondAutBar}\,(M * p)\,d) \cdot P$: if $P$ reduces into $C_2$ in the above sense, if $Q_p$ reduces into $C_2$ in the above sense, if $\mathrm{red}_2 P \notin S_0$, and if $Q_p$ lies in the support of the divisor $\mathrm{heckeDivOneBar}\,h\alpha\,h\beta\,(P \mapsto 1)$, the image of the divisor $P$ under the correspondence given by pullback along `heckeBetaOneBar` followed by pushforward along `heckeAlphaOneBar`, then
--   $$\mathrm{red}_2 Q_p = \mathrm{frobIg} \cdot \mathrm{red}_2 P_2.$$
--
--   The quantification over $\mathrm{Pl}$, $\rho$, $O$, $\pi_k$, $\mathrm{red}_1$, $\mathrm{red}_2$ lies inside the existential quantifiers, so that $d$, $\delta$ and $S_0$ are uniform in the choice of place of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ above $p$ and of the reduction maps.
--
--   This is the place-level Eichler–Shimura relation on the non-Gauss component of the reduction of $X_1(Mp)$ modulo $p$: outside a finite set of places, the $U_p$-partner of a point reducing into the $\sigma$-twisted component is the coefficientwise Frobenius of the reduction of a diamond translate, the diamond itself reducing to a $k$-automorphism $\delta$ of the Igusa function field. It is the geometric input for the subsequent computation of the Hecke and diamond actions on the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the special fibre of the two-component model.
